Mike Fournier <fournim@sympatico.ca> wrote:
>When you mention that there are some more important things to concentrate
>on. Can you possibly elaborate on that.

It's nothing very deep. Weaponry's only one aspect of your character;
once you've got it up to the point where you can kill most things in
your path without any trouble then you're better off concentrating on
the rest of your character rather than micro-optimizing it.

>Also, about the ability to have reflection.  If I find or wish for an Amulet
>of Reflection, am I losing out on some other good amulet that I should be
>using instead?

Life saving can, well, save your life in tricky situations, and
depending on whether you have enough of certain items there's another
amulet you may want to wear for short periods in the endgame. But the
AoR's quite adequate most of the time.

>Why is reflection so important to begin with.
>For armor, right now, I have a used a wish to get a Blessed +2 Gray Dragon
>Scale armor.  Now from what I understand, isn't that Dragon Gray Scale Armor
>a magic resist armor?  My understanding of what Reflection does, is that it
>reflects the magic back at the monster.  So the only difference in having
>this Dragon Gray Scale armor instead, is that I'm not reflecting it back,
>but I am fully resisting the magic. Correct?

That applies to magic, yes, but reflection works on other attacks too:
fire, for example.

>And since I'm duel wielding, the damage I would have reflected back, via the
>Shileld of Reflection, I can just do with my duel wielding swords instead.
>That make sense?
>So unless I'm fully missing the point of Reflection, is it really needed?

The chief benefit of reflection is not so much that it causes damage to
the monster, but that it saves you from damage and destruction of items
in your inventory. A large part of my first death on Astral was that I
forgot about reflection and then lightning attacks blew up my rings and
wands.

By the way, it's "dual", not "duel"; duelling etiquette is rare in
NetHack. :)

